          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#GeneralIdea General
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+of+matter"} phase of matter
            (of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+material"} quantum material
            %em topological
            (sometimes: a
            = succeed ")," do
              %em topological state of matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            bound
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gap"}> gap
            ”) if external deformations of the system that are gentle enough – namely “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+theorem"}> adiabatic
            ” – not to excite modes (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quanta"}> quanta
            )
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt \Delta
            above the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            leave the main properties of the system
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            is concerned with properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            that are invariant under “gentle” – namely “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"}> continuous
            ” – deformations. Indeed, it turns out or is expected that topological phases of matter may be characterized (classified) by certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism+classes"} homeomorphism classes
            or rather by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
            of the kind studied in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            (e.g. the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
          %p
            Such topological phases are fundamentally different from classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phases+of+matter"} phases of matter
            in that they are
            %em not
            controlled by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau+theory+of+phase+transitions"} Landau theory of phase transitions
          %h3#topological_insulators Topological insulators
          %p
            For instance, a
            = succeed "-phase" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystalline
            material (a prime example being
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ExampleGraphene"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+band"} valence band
            of energies occupied by the crystal’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            is separated by such an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gap"} energy gap
            from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conduction+band"} conduction band
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            of the electron’s occupied
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+bundle"} topological vector bundle
            over the material’s
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a non-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+bundle"} trivial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+bundle"} equivariant
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism+class"} homeomorphism class
            %li
              %p
                a non-trivial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+class"} equivariant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+class"} homotopy class
                of its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+map"} classifying map
            %li
              %p
                a non-vanishing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} generalized
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+class"} cohomology class
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+K-theory"} twisted equivariant
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
          %p
            This is part of the statement of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
            (for which there is some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theoretical+physics"} theoretical
            support but which, one should admit, remains a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjecture
          %p
            The idea is that no gentle/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            deformation of such a
            = succeed "-phase" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pressure"} pressure
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+fields"} electric fields
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edge+modes"} edge modes
          %p
            Here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariance
            is with respect to any or all of:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em spatial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+group"} crystallographic point
                symmetries,
            %li
              %p
                %em non-spatial
                symmetries, including:
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CPT+theorem"} CPT symmetries
                    like
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
                %li
                  %p
                    “
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+symmetry"}> internal
                    ” or “on-site” symmetries
                  %p
                    (such as
                    = succeed "-reversal" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
                    and
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-orbit+coupling"} spin-orbit coupling
          %p
            which all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} act
            on:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
                and/or
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} observables
                of the system
              %p
                (eg. on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
                by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
          %p
            If the class of a topological phase of matter crucially depends on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariance
            under such
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries"} symmetries
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+breaking"} break
            some of the symmetry, then one speaks of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phase"} symmetry protected topological phase
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phase"} symmetry protection
          %p
            For instance, without any symmetry protection the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundles"} valence bundles
            in a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+of+matter"} phase
            of a realistic crystalline material (necessarily of effective dimension
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq 3
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
            = succeed "-phase." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulator"} Chern insulator
          %p
            If a more fine-grained topological phase is “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phase"}> protected
            ” by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+group"} crystallographic symmetry
            = succeed "-phase," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+crystalline+insulator"} topological crystalline insulator
          %h3#topological_semimetals Topological semi-metals
          %p
            More generally, there are topological phases where small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            deformations have no effect, as before, but where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %em of possible deformations
            has itself a non-trivial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop"} loop
              of deformations
            may have the effect of having transformed the system’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary operator
            which depends on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+class"} homotopy class
            of this loop.
          %p
            For example, if there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            %em except
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            of “band crossings” or “nodal loci” inside the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gap"} gap
            closes right at the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chemical+potential"} chemical potential
            = succeed "-phase." do
              %em
                topological
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-metal"} semi-metal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            is well-defined (only) on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of these “nodal” points, as before, and as such again invariant under gentle deformations, but now these external deformations (which will gently shift the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+bands"} energy bands
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braiding
            these nodal points around each other then it may in total have the effect of having transformed the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            by an operation in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representation"} braid group representation
          %p
            If this braiding is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
            around the nodal points is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of states, all of the ground state energy at the chemical potential, whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            or that its ground states exhibits “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short-range+entanglement"}> long-range entanglement
            ” (at least “no
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short-range+entanglement"} short-range entanglement
          %h3#TopologicalFieldTheory Topological field theory
          %p
            Therefore, in this case of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            of the topological material, say as the nodal points are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braided
            around each other, is characterized by:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
              %p (the degenerate ground state),
            %li
              %p
                a vanishing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
              %p (being the restriction of the full Hamiltonian of the material to its energy=0 eigenstates),
            %li
              %p
                global dynamics depending (only) on the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"}> topology
                ” (really: the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              %p
                (such as on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid
                formed by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
                of the nodal points).
          %p
            These are exactly the characteristics of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            of the type of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that describes all the higher excitations of the given material (which is at least as rich as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} Coulomb potential
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nuclei"} atomic nuclei
            on the crystal sites, see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Dirac+field#FreeDiracFieldInCoulombBackground"} here
          %h3#anyons Anyons
          %p
            Generally, it is expected that
            = succeed "=2" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            in a topological phase of matter exhibit such a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+order"}> symmetry protected
            )
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            in that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braiding
            of them around each other inside the topological host material is described by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            and in particular constitutes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representation"} braid group representation
            on the systems
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
          %p
            In this case one refers to these defects also a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"} anyons
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} CS-WZW correspondence
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            then predicts that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
            that constitute these anyonic ground states in topological phases are given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
          %p
            The idea to use the resulting monodromy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representations"} braid representations
            constituted by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computer"} quantum computer
            underlies the subject of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum computation
          %p
            The technical problem with implementing this and related ideas is that, in general, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            of a topological phase of matter is small, so that it is first of all hard to detect and second it will be hard to
            %em preserve
            (hence hard to remain in the topological phase) as one tries to put the “topological properties” of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+material"} quantum material
            to use.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#quantum_hall_effect Quantum Hall effect
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Hall+effect"}> quantum Hall effect
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+spin+Hall+effect"} quantum spin Hall effect
          %h3#ExampleGraphene Graphene
          %p
            The first and archetypical example of all these phenomena is seen in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            %em almost
            closes over two nodal
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+points"} Dirac points
            (up to a tiny shift by the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-orbit+coupling"} spin-orbit coupling
          %p
            The topological phase/order of graphene is “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phase"}> symmetry protected
            ”:
          %p
            That this must be the case follows already from the fact that any plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+bundle"} complex vector bundle
            (such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            may naively seem to be) is necessarily
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+bundle"} trivial
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of two Dirac points inside the Brillouin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-torus"} 2-torus
            (a punctured torus, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus#AsAHomotopyType"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            in graphene is “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T I
            ”-symmetric, which forces the valence bundle to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+bundle"} real vector bundle
            with a class not in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU-theory"} KU-theory
            but in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-theory"} KO-theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M%C3%B6bius+strip"} Möbius strip
            = succeed "-symmetric" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+phases"} Berry phases
            around the two nodal points.
          %h3#haldane_model Haldane model
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haldane+model"} Haldane model
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+of+matter"}> phase of matter
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+material"} quantum material
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gapped+Hamiltonian"} gapped Hamiltonian
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unstable+topological+phases+of+matter"} unstable topological phases of matter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+network+state"} tensor network state
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Hall+effect"} quantum Hall effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+spin+liquid"} quantum spin liquid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strange+metal"} strange metal
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+semimetal"} Weyl semimetal
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metamaterials"} metamaterials
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+photonics"} topological photonics
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phononics"} topological phononics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computing"} topological quantum computing
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+spin+Hall+effect"} quantum spin Hall effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
          %h2#references References
          %h3#reviews Reviews
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sanju+Gupta"}> Sanju Gupta
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Avadh+Saxena"} Avadh Saxena
                (eds.):
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Role of Topology in Materials
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 189
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-319-76596-9"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-76596-9
            %li#Vanderbilt18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Vanderbilt"}> David Vanderbilt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Berry Phases in Electronic Structure Theory – Electric Polarization, Orbital Magnetization and Topological Insulators
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316662205"} doi:10.1017/9781316662205
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bei+Zeng"}> Bei Zeng
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xie+Chen"} Xie Chen
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duan-Lu+Zhou"} Duan-Lu Zhou
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xiao-Gang+Wen"} Xiao-Gang Wen
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Information+Meets+Quantum+Matter"} Quantum Information Meets Quantum Matter
                  – From Quantum Entanglement to Topological Phases of Many-Body Systems
                , Quantum Science and Technology (QST), Springer (2019)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02595"} arXiv:1508.02595
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4939-9084-9"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4939-9084-9
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tudor+D.+Stanescu"}> Tudor D. Stanescu
                , Part II of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to Topological Quantum Matter &amp; Quantum Computation
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.routledge.com/Introduction-to-Topological-Quantum-Matter--Quantum-Computation/Stanescu/p/book/9780367574116"} ISBN:9780367574116
          %p Review and survey:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Shou-cheng Zhang,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Viewpoint: Topological states of quantum matter
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1103/Physics.1.6"} doi:10.1103/Physics.1.6
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ching-Kai+Chiu"}> Ching-Kai Chiu
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffrey+C.Y.+Teo"} Jeffrey C.Y. Teo
